 that an experiment was run.\"; `documents.track.md` = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- Verified just before this handoff: `ls -la /workspace/` shows the same single file; `python3 --version` = `Python 3.12.3`; `import jax` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`; `/proc/cpuinfo` reports 24 logical processors.\n- Sandbox envelope (F): bubblewrap, network disabled, GPU disabled, CPU affinity 24, system Python only, project dependencies not preinstalled. `public_task.note`: \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"\n\n(b) Reasoning/rationale for the design choices, kept distinct from the observations above:\n- The four sub-claims (asymptotic, env-step, wall-clock, cross-map) decompose a single vector comparison into four scalar answerable questions so that an apparent \"X is faster than Y\" claim is forced to specify *faster on which axis*.\n- Random arm is included to sanity-check the eval pipeline; without it, a comparison where both arms look indistinguishable could be either a real null result or a broken pipeline.\n- Paired seeds across arms give within-map interference reduction, which is the only axis where blocking is structurally available; cross-map pairing is not done because maps have independent randomness sources.\n- Three logged time axes (wall-clock, env-step, updates) are reported because PPO and Q-Learning differ structurally in throughput; either metric alone could mislead.\n- Pre-registered threshold rule `T = 0.7 \u00d7 better-arm mean final-window return` is designed so the threshold is set by a deterministic, blindable reduction of the same data, with a fallback to \"neither reached\" reported as a distinguishable outcome to avoid censoring-to-zero bias.\n- Holm\u2013Bonferroni over 12 tests (4 sub-claims \u00d7 3 maps) controls family-wise error at the headline-comparison level; if a stricter correction is preferred, the design is a one-line change at result-review.\n- Scope-reduction f